WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:10.830
Thanks for having me. I think since we're in a classroom

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.009
setting, as Mike said, we can make this very interactive. I'

00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:18.429
ll make a few opening remarks and sketch out what we did and

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:20.959
we did it as quickly as I can with the clock back there

00:00:20.959 --> 00:00:23.959
reminding me what time it is and then would love to take

00:00:23.959 --> 00:00:27.689
your questions on any aspects of this. I'll start at the end

00:00:27.709 --> 00:00:30.519
actually because when GM went public that day I had agreed

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:37.319
to co-host CNBC and was sitting on the set of CNBC as the

00:00:37.339 --> 00:00:40.749
various GM executives and the floor people and bell rang and

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:44.419
the first share was traded and all that, and it was an

00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:47.919
amazing moment after all the ups and downs and all the work

00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:52.009
and all the effort even though we had felt very good about

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.919
what we had done to actually see it validated by the market

00:00:54.919 --> 00:00:58.559
was really quite a moment.

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:03.119
Before I go back in time, I do want to just amplify on Mike'

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.629
s recognition of Clay Calhoun who is sitting over here

00:01:05.629 --> 00:01:08.750
because you'll all want to read this book, because Clay is

00:01:08.769 --> 00:01:11.469
very prominently mentioned in it. He is actually in the

00:01:11.489 --> 00:01:16.450
index, but I want to just read one paragraph. This came up

00:01:16.469 --> 00:01:19.709
in the middle of the frenzy when we were trying to get

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:22.239
Chrysler and Fiat all on the same page and they had

00:01:22.239 --> 00:01:27.140
different sense of projections and we basically said, "You

00:01:27.159 --> 00:01:30.439
guys have a weekend to figure this out," and I didn't want

00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:33.209
to spend the weekend in Detroit. Ron Bloom, my deputy, didn'

00:01:33.209 --> 00:01:39.140
t want to spend the weekend in Detroit so we sent Clay and

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:43.069
another fella called Brian Osias, so this little paragraph

00:01:43.089 --> 00:01:47.390
begins on Saturday morning: "By 11 am on Saturday morning,

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:50.689
Osias and Calhoun convened more than two dozen Fiat and

00:01:50.709 --> 00:01:53.420
Chrysler executives and advisors around the large table in

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:56.700
Chrysler's 15th floor boardroom. The executives were almost

00:01:56.719 --> 00:02:00.569
all middle-aged industry veterans. Osias was 32-years old

00:02:00.589 --> 00:02:04.359
and Calhoun was 26 and both looked younger than their years.

00:02:04.379 --> 00:02:06.579
We're going to sit at this table until we're done, Calhoun

00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:11.289
announced." There is much more about Clay here. You should

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:15.879
all get this book and read it. I'm sorry Clay, I didn't mean

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:20.099
to embarrass you like that but Clay and I worked together at

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.239
Quad Wrangle and he was a superstar there and so when I went

00:02:24.259 --> 00:02:29.009
to Washington to take this job and he asked me if we were

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.030
looking for help and I said, "We're looking for all kinds of

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.030
help," and I was really delighted that he joined us and like

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.780
all the rest of our team he did a fabulous job.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.429
Let me, just as I said, try to do two things in a short

00:02:41.449 --> 00:02:44.649
amount of time and so I'll speak reasonably quickly and then

00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:47.729
we can open it up. Let me just give you a quick chronology

00:02:47.729 --> 00:02:51.379
for those of you who didn't follow all this all that closely

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.900
and I can't blame you for not and then, let me give you a

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.269
few of the takeaways from the project that I would love you

00:02:58.289 --> 00:03:02.189
to all leave the room thinking about. And then, as I said,

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:05.209
we can have a discussion. I think probably the most of you

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:08.379
remember that sort of first big public event in the auto

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.629
crisis in November of 2008 when the three executives flew to

00:03:12.649 --> 00:03:14.870
Washington to testify in front of the Congress in their

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.939
separate planes. That's the part everybody remembers. What

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:22.819
people don't remember as well is they showed up having GM

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.959
and Chrysler particularly having kind of frankly suddenly

00:03:25.979 --> 00:03:28.419
discovered that they were about to run out of money, having

00:03:28.419 --> 00:03:31.209
just as suddenly told the Bush administration that they were

00:03:31.229 --> 00:03:35.029
running out of money and then being sent to Congress by

00:03:35.049 --> 00:03:38.829
Secretary Paulson and appearing there with essentially no

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:42.449
plan, no idea what they were going to do with money and no

00:03:42.469 --> 00:03:48.179
recognition of any sort of culpability in the fact that they

00:03:48.199 --> 00:03:50.129
had run out of money.

00:03:50.149 --> 00:03:51.549
There was a lot of

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:53.609
[inaudible] at the end of 2008. Obviously, there had been a

00:03:53.609 --> 00:03:56.789
presidential election, it was a lame duck administration.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.829
There was a banking crisis going on but President Bush and

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:03.919
Congress ultimately just failed to act. They just couldn't

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:07.059
pull themselves together to do anything about autos at that

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:12.369
point. President Bush, in my opinion, quite correctly made a

00:04:12.389 --> 00:04:15.649
decision that the auto companies were not going to fail on

00:04:15.669 --> 00:04:19.340
his watch and so one of this kind of SWAT teams of ex-

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.439
Goldman guys that he brought in sort of pulled together in

00:04:23.459 --> 00:04:29.910
December of 2008 and provided bridge loans of about $17

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:34.930
billion to GM and to Chrysler to get them over to the end of

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:38.000
year, get them into 2009 when the new team was going to

00:04:38.019 --> 00:04:42.160
arrive and would hopefully then have the time to band with

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.519
just kind of the energy frankly to address the problem. And

00:04:45.539 --> 00:04:49.910
as I said, I think given the situation at that point, the

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.970
end of an eight year presidency, a lot of very tired people,

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.490
I think the Bush people did about as good a job as you can

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.660
do under the circumstances.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.170
President elect Obama then of course realized that he had to

00:05:03.189 --> 00:05:08.680
deal with this problem and after some deliberation decided

00:05:08.699 --> 00:05:12.939
they would set up a separate task force to do this. There

00:05:12.959 --> 00:05:15.949
were two reasons really for that. One, they knew that the

00:05:15.969 --> 00:05:20.519
economic and financial crisis was going to occupy all of the

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:23.509
people who had the traditional jobs of under-secretary of

00:05:23.529 --> 00:05:25.920
this or assistant-secretary of that within treasury, within

00:05:25.939 --> 00:05:28.769
commerce, within the White House. And the second reason they

00:05:28.789 --> 00:05:31.939
did it, which is an interesting reflection on how our

00:05:31.959 --> 00:05:35.959
government works is that we had no auto expertise in the

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:38.469
government really. For that matter, we didn't have any

00:05:38.469 --> 00:05:41.530
industrial expertise and I don't say that critically, but in

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.430
this country relative to some European countries and other

00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:48.689
countries we really have tried to stay out as a government

00:05:48.709 --> 00:05:52.910
from the industrial sector and so part of that is not really

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:57.699
having people whose job it is to understand industry in

00:05:57.719 --> 00:06:00.420
general or even specific industries. And so, there are a

00:06:00.439 --> 00:06:02.939
couple of people on the Commerce Department who kind of

00:06:02.939 --> 00:06:05.939
monitored auto imports and we later found out that there are

00:06:05.959 --> 00:06:09.030
a few people over at the FED who actually were pretty good

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:12.740
at forecasting auto sales in general but there was really

00:06:12.759 --> 00:06:14.709
nobody in the entire government who knew anything about

00:06:14.709 --> 00:06:18.740
autos and of course I didn't know anything about autos

00:06:18.759 --> 00:06:21.269
either but that's a different matter.

00:06:21.289 --> 00:06:24.829
So they decided to set up this team and they did come to me,

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:28.129
and so you may say why did they come to me, and that would

00:06:28.129 --> 00:06:33.930
be a very valid question. The short answer is because Tim

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:37.610
Geitner and Larry Summers; the two guys who Clay and I and

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:41.040
the rest of our team worked for directly as well as

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:43.629
obviously indirectly with President they had the view that

00:06:43.649 --> 00:06:46.419
this was not a management job. We were not being hired to

00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:49.420
run these companies, we were being hired to decide what the

00:06:49.439 --> 00:06:52.459
next new car should be or what cars they should make, it was

00:06:52.459 --> 00:06:56.370
really a financial restructuring exercise and a private

00:06:56.389 --> 00:06:58.959
equity exercise for that matter. We were being hired to

00:06:58.979 --> 00:07:02.240
basically look at these companies and figure out how to take

00:07:02.259 --> 00:07:07.649
them from the disastrous place they were and restructure

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:10.850
them into something that could be viable, and by the way, we

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:13.649
knew that we were going to be making a very significant

00:07:13.669 --> 00:07:17.439
additional investment of government money and so it was a

00:07:17.459 --> 00:07:20.709
private equity exercise in the sense that we were investing

00:07:20.729 --> 00:07:23.979
all of your money, tax payers' money and we needed to try to

00:07:23.979 --> 00:07:28.310
figure out whether this was a good investment or not and be

00:07:28.329 --> 00:07:30.930
able to tell the President and his other senior advisors in

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:36.709
the White House what we thought was likely to happen.

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:41.170
They looked at me and Clay and the rest of us who ultimately

00:07:41.189 --> 00:07:44.029
arrived as a restructuring team. I was not the most

00:07:44.049 --> 00:07:46.920
experienced restructuring guy in the world either but

00:07:46.939 --> 00:07:50.879
because of my political involvement they felt that since

00:07:50.899 --> 00:07:52.979
this was going to be an exercise that occurred in the

00:07:52.979 --> 00:07:57.199
political arena, even though there was a great commitment to

00:07:57.219 --> 00:08:00.550
having it being done in a thoughtful way that having someone

00:08:00.569 --> 00:08:05.680
who had a sense of the politics of it all would be helpful

00:08:05.699 --> 00:08:10.329
as well as the hopefully the financial skills. We were on an

00:08:10.349 --> 00:08:14.019
incredibly tight timeframe because as I said, they had been

00:08:14.039 --> 00:08:17.680
given a relatively small amount of money, $17 billion to get

00:08:17.699 --> 00:08:21.189
them through essentially the first quarter of 2009 and

00:08:21.209 --> 00:08:25.629
through the end of March, so I didn't really even start

00:08:25.649 --> 00:08:30.089
doing anything until January of 2009. My appointment wasn't

00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:33.939
announced until late February of 2009. The rest of the team

00:08:33.959 --> 00:08:43.659
really didn't come together until early March of 2009 and so

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:47.210
we were operating on this really tight timeframe, tighter

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:51.519
than any project I think I have ever worked on and with a

00:08:51.539 --> 00:08:55.879
relatively small team. We ended up with 14 of us; mostly

00:08:55.899 --> 00:08:58.009
people like Clay and me who had come from Wall Street,

00:08:58.009 --> 00:09:02.289
because they wanted to serve but some policy people as well

00:09:02.309 --> 00:09:06.389
and that by the standards of what we were being asked to do

00:09:06.409 --> 00:09:08.819
was an exceptionally small team. I remember one of my

00:09:08.819 --> 00:09:12.980
reservations about taking the job was how are we going to

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.250
find the people and get the people we needed to do this and

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.250
Larry Summers said to me in one of my early meetings, "How

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:22.250
many people do you think you need?" and I said, "In a

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:25.819
private sector context I think 50. Here, maybe 15," and he

00:09:25.839 --> 00:09:28.980
said, "My whole staff is only 19." And I said, "Well, that

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.049
may be but we need 15 to do this job," so we ended up having

00:09:33.069 --> 00:09:36.889
14. We got essentially what we needed but it was, as I said,

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:40.319
I think JP Morgan you mentioned, David Hopper was working on

00:09:40.339 --> 00:09:42.699
this and Morgan Stanley and they had dozens and dozens of

00:09:42.699 --> 00:09:46.960
people, so it was a very lean team.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:50.490
From the beginning, there were two principles that the

00:09:50.509 --> 00:09:53.620
President, Larry Summers and Tim Geitner decided that were

00:09:53.639 --> 00:10:00.009
really critical to the success of our efforts. The first was

00:10:00.029 --> 00:10:04.029
that the only money that we wanted to put in and hoped to

00:10:04.049 --> 00:10:07.350
put in was in the context of a fundamental restructure and

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:11.389
make the companies viable. They decided and I was really

00:10:11.409 --> 00:10:14.220
pleased that they did that they didn't want to kick the can

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.209
down the road anymore, they didn't want to try to paper it

00:10:17.209 --> 00:10:20.209
over, they didn't want to try to just get to the next

00:10:20.209 --> 00:10:22.950
election. Whatever we did, they wanted it to be something

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:26.080
that made these companies viable again. And the second

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:29.519
important principle that we established early on and they

00:10:29.539 --> 00:10:34.330
stood by throughout this was shared sacrifice, that this was

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:37.850
not going to be done on the backs of the UAW alone, it was

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:41.340
not going to be done on the backs of the creditors alone or

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:44.830
on the dealers or the suppliers but that we were going to

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:47.600
try to get everybody to the table and get everybody to

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.889
recognize that they were not the only ones who were being

00:10:50.909 --> 00:10:54.199
asked to sacrifice. And both of those principles turned out

00:10:54.219 --> 00:10:57.439
to be very important ones.

00:10:57.459 --> 00:11:01.590
We plunged in. As I have acknowledged, I didn't know

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.429
anything about autos, so we approached this with fresh eyes.

00:11:04.449 --> 00:11:07.939
We've had every expert wanted to come and tell us their

00:11:07.959 --> 00:11:11.519
views about the auto industry. We met with an awful lot of

00:11:11.539 --> 00:11:14.269
them, we listened to everybody's thoughts, we pushed back.

00:11:14.289 --> 00:11:17.259
We certainly spent time with the companies. This is all sort

00:11:17.279 --> 00:11:21.220
of now in March of 2009 because we knew that by the end of

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.990
March, the President had to tell the country what he was

00:11:24.009 --> 00:11:29.769
going to do about the auto situation. And by March 25th or

00:11:29.789 --> 00:11:33.519
so we had pretty much the essential elements ready for the

00:11:33.539 --> 00:11:35.949
President to decide.

00:11:35.969 --> 00:11:38.809
The toughest decision that the President had to make really

00:11:38.829 --> 00:11:42.870
was whether to save Chrysler or not. We knew pretty well

00:11:42.889 --> 00:11:47.710
that we were going to save GM. GM was too big, too important

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:52.710
and what we've thought of as a real company that needed to

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:56.370
be saved. Chrysler was a tougher call. It was, as you know

00:11:56.389 --> 00:12:01.789
probably the third largest and third of three American auto

00:12:01.809 --> 00:12:05.639
companies. It was only a North American player, it had been

00:12:05.659 --> 00:12:08.699
owned for seven years by Daimler and then for a couple of

00:12:08.719 --> 00:12:11.759
years by Cerberus and had been pretty well hollowed out. It

00:12:11.779 --> 00:12:15.029
didn't have single car that was on consumer report's most

00:12:15.049 --> 00:12:19.399
recommended list and there was a strong part of our group

00:12:19.419 --> 00:12:23.980
that actually made a very passionate and very logical and

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.360
analytically sound argument that Chrysler should be left to

00:12:26.379 --> 00:12:30.299
fail, that what was capitalism about if not letting weak

00:12:30.319 --> 00:12:33.779
companies fail, why was the government putting a lot of

00:12:33.799 --> 00:12:36.679
money into an old industry company and I guess I should

00:12:36.699 --> 00:12:40.439
recognize that I am in the part of new industry land here

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:43.459
and so maybe you all don't think we shouldn't have saved any

00:12:43.459 --> 00:12:46.720
auto companies and put the money in Google instead, but

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:50.429
there was that argument that if the government has limited

00:12:50.449 --> 00:12:53.769
dollars which of course it has, they should be allocated to

00:12:53.789 --> 00:12:58.009
newer industries that might have greater growth prospects.

00:12:58.029 --> 00:13:00.639
On the other side of the argument, the economy was losing

00:13:00.639 --> 00:13:03.740
six or seven hundred thousand jobs a month at this point.

00:13:03.759 --> 00:13:07.409
The immediate impact of Chrysler shutting down would've been

00:13:07.429 --> 00:13:10.340
around three hundred thousand jobs lost; only small part of

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:13.039
those from Chrysler itself but you then had suppliers, you

00:13:13.059 --> 00:13:15.809
had dealers, you had all the people who have serviced this

00:13:15.809 --> 00:13:20.689
company and so on and while I don't believe that we would've

00:13:20.709 --> 00:13:24.360
saved Chrysler simply because of those jobs, as I said, if

00:13:24.379 --> 00:13:28.069
we determined that Chrysler was not viable I think the

00:13:28.089 --> 00:13:30.769
President would've agreed with a recommendation to let it

00:13:30.789 --> 00:13:36.019
liquidate but after a lot of hard work, I think everybody on

00:13:36.039 --> 00:13:39.149
the team agreed that Chrysler could be viable and that the

00:13:39.169 --> 00:13:41.809
government could give its money back from Chrysler. It was

00:13:41.829 --> 00:13:44.929
more a question of whether it was something that government

00:13:44.949 --> 00:13:47.659
should do. Among the points that the people who thought

00:13:47.679 --> 00:13:52.399
Chrysler should be allowed to go made was that if Chrysler

00:13:52.419 --> 00:13:56.079
went out of business, what we learned as we learned about

00:13:56.099 --> 00:13:58.769
the industry was that a disproportionate amount of Chrysler

00:13:58.789 --> 00:14:02.620
sales would've gone to Ford and GM relative to the foreign

00:14:02.639 --> 00:14:05.480
companies, because the product line up of Chrysler minivans

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:08.000
and trucks and things like that were they were strongest

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.710
lined the up best with Ford and GM. In effect, we knew we

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.979
were going to be a big shareholder in GM, or a big investor

00:14:14.979 --> 00:14:17.469
anyway. By letting Chrysler go, we would make the value of

00:14:17.469 --> 00:14:21.159
that investment greater but of course then you'd have all

00:14:21.179 --> 00:14:24.100
those jobs lost, so it was a very passionate, hard-thought

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:27.220
debate that went really, as I said, right to the President

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:32.209
and to two meetings in the White House on the 25th or 26th

00:14:32.229 --> 00:14:36.149
of March and at the end of hearing it all, the President

00:14:36.169 --> 00:14:39.149
came down where a slight majority of our group had come

00:14:39.169 --> 00:14:43.769
down, which was to provide Chrysler with additional money if

00:14:43.789 --> 00:14:47.240
it could meet a series of very tough tests in terms of

00:14:47.259 --> 00:14:51.090
concessions, in terms of a business plan that we could back,

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:53.679
in terms of an alliance with Fiat because we didn't feel

00:14:53.679 --> 00:14:56.409
that Chrysler could exist simply as a North American player;

00:14:56.429 --> 00:15:00.179
we felt that it needed a global alliance and Fiat was the

00:15:00.199 --> 00:15:02.919
company that had come forward that we thought made the most

00:15:02.919 --> 00:15:06.939
sense. And so, it was still not a done deal but at least all

00:15:06.959 --> 00:15:11.409
things being accomplished, the President agreed that we

00:15:11.429 --> 00:15:15.079
would save Chrysler.

00:15:15.099 --> 00:15:18.100
So, he gave the speech on March 30th that probably all of

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:22.189
you remember in addition to the announcement about Chrysler

00:15:22.209 --> 00:15:25.289
and the announcement that we were going to try to save GM

00:15:25.309 --> 00:15:28.970
but still had worked to do. He also announced that Rick

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:33.649
Wagner was going to stepping down as chairman of GM. This,

00:15:33.669 --> 00:15:37.659
to us, was the most obvious decision that the President had

00:15:37.679 --> 00:15:43.269
to make. GM had lost $30 billion of cash in the 15 months of

00:15:43.289 --> 00:15:48.090
2008 and the first quarter of 2009. As I said earlier, when

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:51.309
Rick appeared in front of the Senate in November of 2008

00:15:51.329 --> 00:15:54.809
there was no plan, there was no sense of responsibility. It

00:15:54.829 --> 00:15:58.370
didn't get any better. The plan that GM submitted to us in

00:15:58.389 --> 00:16:02.189
February of 2009 still didn't show us that they had a sense

00:16:02.209 --> 00:16:04.519
as to how they were going to become profitable and viable

00:16:04.519 --> 00:16:08.519
again and we simply believed that under those circumstances,

00:16:08.539 --> 00:16:12.179
there needed to be a change in management. Rick is a very

00:16:12.199 --> 00:16:15.289
decent hard-working smart guy who is totally dedicated to

00:16:15.309 --> 00:16:18.769
the company but we were thinking about this in a private

00:16:18.789 --> 00:16:22.549
equity context in which management really does matter and so

00:16:22.569 --> 00:16:24.829
we felt and recommended to the President that there be a

00:16:24.829 --> 00:16:28.629
change in management and with really almost no discussion,

00:16:28.649 --> 00:16:32.889
which is not meant as any disrespect to Rick, the President

00:16:32.909 --> 00:16:36.419
agreed with that. Mike was nice enough to hand out an

00:16:36.439 --> 00:16:38.939
article I wrote recently about the role of management which

00:16:38.939 --> 00:16:41.529
related to Steve Jobs' last leave of absence and I'm happy

00:16:41.529 --> 00:16:44.349
to talk about that a bit in the Q &amp; A because one of the

00:16:44.349 --> 00:16:47.279
things I've always believed in both the investment banking

00:16:47.299 --> 00:16:50.259
and the private equity business is that the jockey is as

00:16:50.279 --> 00:16:53.049
important as the horse, that it's important to have a good

00:16:53.049 --> 00:16:57.949
business but who the CEO and who the top management team are

00:16:57.969 --> 00:17:02.639
is also important and so we felt that we could not really

00:17:02.659 --> 00:17:05.559
recommend in good conscience to the President putting more

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:10.849
money into GM unless there was a change in management.

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:13.819
So, as everybody knows we ended up putting both companies

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:17.389
into bankruptcy. A lot of folks said it couldn't be done, a

00:17:17.409 --> 00:17:21.420
lot of folks said that we broke bankruptcy law or we made

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:24.799
new law or the heavy hand of government, I'm happy to talk

00:17:24.819 --> 00:17:28.750
about all that but needless to say, we believe that

00:17:28.769 --> 00:17:32.569
everything we did was in conformity with the law. In fact,

00:17:32.589 --> 00:17:35.589
it was litigated all the way to the Supreme Court. I don't

00:17:35.589 --> 00:17:38.589
think when I went to Washington, I ever imagined that I was

00:17:38.589 --> 00:17:41.339
going to be worrying about Supreme Court decision as part of

00:17:41.339 --> 00:17:44.589
my job but there we were, and at the end, it was all

00:17:44.589 --> 00:17:47.839
approved in every step of the way and we got GM and Chrysler

00:17:47.849 --> 00:17:51.490
in and out of bankruptcy in essentially record time; 42 days

00:17:51.509 --> 00:17:58.889
for Chrysler and 39 days for GM.

00:17:58.909 --> 00:18:02.470
I think, as you know, since then it's worked out quite well.

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:06.109
We talked about the GM IPO briefly but the GM IPO simply

00:18:06.129 --> 00:18:10.589
symbolized the recovery that the two companies have made.

00:18:10.609 --> 00:18:13.660
The bankruptcy process was really essential to giving these

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:18.129
companies a fresh start. In the case of GM, we took their

00:18:18.149 --> 00:18:22.839
liabilities from $120 billion to $55 billion. We took $8

00:18:22.859 --> 00:18:27.339
billion a year of annual operating cost of their North

00:18:27.359 --> 00:18:30.460
America division and this was all accomplished by the kind

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:33.730
of shared sacrifice that I alluded to before in which

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:38.599
everybody came to the table willing to make grudgingly. It

00:18:38.619 --> 00:18:42.099
was hard thought, it was difficult but in the end willing to

00:18:42.119 --> 00:18:43.119
make changes.

00:18:43.119 --> 00:18:45.569
I remember one of our earlier meetings with the UAW when Ron

00:18:45.569 --> 00:18:49.589
Gettelfinger who was then the president basically said to us

00:18:49.609 --> 00:18:52.220
and actually this was in Detroit, at Solidarity House said

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:56.509
to us, "Look, we're willing to do something here because I,

00:18:56.529 --> 00:19:01.099
Ron Gettelfinger understand that if we don't cooperate these

00:19:01.119 --> 00:19:04.059
companies may disappear but we feel like we've been to

00:19:04.079 --> 00:19:07.639
sacrifice in the past," which they had, "and we feel like we

00:19:07.659 --> 00:19:10.659
are the only ones who've been asked to sacrifice and we want

00:19:10.659 --> 00:19:13.659
to see other people asked to sacrifice." And so, it was that

00:19:13.659 --> 00:19:17.200
concept of shared sacrifice that allowed us to make more

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:21.389
progress with the cost structure, the liability structure

00:19:21.409 --> 00:19:24.339
these companies then people haven't been able to make

00:19:24.359 --> 00:19:27.399
before. A lot of it relates back to that famous saying of

00:19:27.419 --> 00:19:31.629
Rahm Emanuel's which actually was not his but he said it

00:19:31.649 --> 00:19:34.250
first, but I can't remember which is, "Never let a crisis go

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:37.420
to waste," that we had this atmosphere of crisis, we had

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:41.450
everybody realizing that without pulling together and making

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:46.710
this work, it would be a disaster. And so, we were fortunate

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:48.409
to have that going for us.

00:19:48.429 --> 00:19:53.329
One of the other principles that we established early on was

00:19:53.349 --> 00:19:56.490
that the government was going to get in and get out as

00:19:56.509 --> 00:19:59.509
quickly as possible, it was going to be kind of the opposite

00:19:59.509 --> 00:20:02.509
of Vietnam. We didn't want to be mired in the car business

00:20:02.509 --> 00:20:05.639
and so we established early on private sector boards for

00:20:05.659 --> 00:20:11.420
these companies and we really looked for directors who had

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:15.609
exactly the right skills. It was in an effort to create a

00:20:15.629 --> 00:20:18.839
sort of Noah's Ark compendium of two of everything, it was

00:20:18.859 --> 00:20:21.829
an effort to put together a mix of former CEOs and private

00:20:21.829 --> 00:20:25.269
equity people who really could oversee these companies and

00:20:25.289 --> 00:20:28.409
so as you see, GM and Chrysler going forward now, both very

00:20:28.429 --> 00:20:32.299
successfully, a lot of the credit goes not only to the new

00:20:32.319 --> 00:20:35.990
management teams of the two companies but also to the new

00:20:36.009 --> 00:20:38.930
boards of directors who have been really tough and have just

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:43.119
instilled a whole new aura of corporate governance at these

00:20:43.139 --> 00:20:46.700
companies. GM in particular had terrible corporate

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:50.430
governance. One of our colleagues liked to; I shouldn't say

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:53.700
this in front of the cameras but liked to speak of the GM

00:20:53.700 --> 00:21:00.879
directors in very disparaging terms and happily, we as I

00:21:00.899 --> 00:21:03.829
said made those changes. And the new management teams have

00:21:03.849 --> 00:21:06.450
done a great job. A lot of these old industry practices,

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:11.119
some of which actually had logic in terms of the structural

00:21:11.139 --> 00:21:14.000
problems in the industry led the companies to do things that

00:21:14.019 --> 00:21:16.669
made no sense like have too much inventory on the dealers'

00:21:16.669 --> 00:21:20.180
lots, like the famous incentive as in the 0 financing and

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:22.950
all that stuff where they couldn't make money in the end.

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.950
There were reasons why they did all that, so it wasn't as

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:28.700
crazy as it seemed but by changing their balance sheets and

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:32.549
changing their cost structures, we essentially gave them a

00:21:32.569 --> 00:21:35.839
reason to operate in a more logical and rational business

00:21:35.859 --> 00:21:38.700
way and that's what they are doing. Inventories are under

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:42.619
control, incentives are under control and most importantly,

00:21:42.639 --> 00:21:45.740
through the restructuring we dramatically changed to the

00:21:45.759 --> 00:21:49.529
break even of these companies. So GM, prior to restructuring

00:21:49.549 --> 00:21:52.549
really couldn't make money unless 16 or 17 million cars a

00:21:52.569 --> 00:21:55.339
year was sold in this country which is something that's only

00:21:55.339 --> 00:21:58.899
occurred a few times in our history. After the

00:21:58.919 --> 00:22:03.339
restructuring, GM could make money in a 10 or 11 million car

00:22:03.359 --> 00:22:06.909
sale environment and so standing here today, with car sales

00:22:06.929 --> 00:22:10.210
running about 12.5 million, they were actually 11.5 million

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:13.759
for last year, GM last year had its first annual profit

00:22:13.779 --> 00:22:19.079
since 2004 and its highest profit since 1999. So even with

00:22:19.099 --> 00:22:24.000
car sales still very depressed, GM is making substantial net

00:22:24.019 --> 00:22:26.289
income and Chrysler is actually pretty good operating

00:22:26.309 --> 00:22:30.599
profits although not yet net income.

00:22:30.619 --> 00:22:33.859
And I continue to be very optimistic. If for no other very

00:22:33.879 --> 00:22:38.329
simple reason that we need to sell 15 million cars a year in

00:22:38.349 --> 00:22:40.879
this country simply to keep the fleet from aging. In other

00:22:40.879 --> 00:22:44.389
words, to deal with replacing cars on the road plus a small

00:22:44.409 --> 00:22:47.579
addition of new drivers every year it's roughly a 15 million

00:22:47.599 --> 00:22:51.470
car sale rate. For the last three years, we've been below

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:57.599
that, well below that in 2008 and so, this is the first time

00:22:57.619 --> 00:23:00.829
in history that essentially more cars have been scrapped

00:23:00.849 --> 00:23:05.029
than have actually been sold. So that whole situation is not

00:23:05.049 --> 00:23:09.549
a long term sustainable one in my opinion and I think car

00:23:09.569 --> 00:23:12.200
sales will continue to climb. I think they'll be somewhere

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.950
between 13 and 14 million this year and then just keep

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:18.389
going, and given the changes in the cost structure of these

00:23:18.409 --> 00:23:21.849
companies, they can make a lot of money as GM already has as

00:23:21.869 --> 00:23:25.240
car sales go up even just a little bit because of the

00:23:25.259 --> 00:23:28.680
operating leverage that's inherent to them.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:32.389
Let me quickly run through what I think are the takeaways,

00:23:32.409 --> 00:23:35.049
if you kind of want to leave the room with a thought about

00:23:35.049 --> 00:23:39.149
what does this all mean. This is what I think it all means

00:23:39.169 --> 00:23:42.970
and I think I have about six of them. First, that this

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:45.460
really could not have happened without the President's

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:50.460
insistence on shared sacrifice and only putting money in if

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.950
the companies could be viable. That is not intended to be a

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:56.909
political statement or endorsement or whatever. I'm off the

00:23:56.929 --> 00:23:59.629
government payroll, that's what I actually believe that

00:23:59.649 --> 00:24:03.019
without that kind of support in the face of what a lot of

00:24:03.039 --> 00:24:05.539
people thought would be an enormous pressure from labor, for

00:24:05.539 --> 00:24:08.809
example, to be less tough, I don't think we could've done

00:24:08.829 --> 00:24:10.859
this.

00:24:10.879 --> 00:24:13.769
Secondly, we were able to do this; for that reason, we were

00:24:13.769 --> 00:24:16.819
able to do this just almost exactly as if we were in the

00:24:16.819 --> 00:24:19.569
private sector. As I said, we approached this as a private

00:24:19.569 --> 00:24:21.869
equity investment exercise, we made all the same kind of

00:24:21.869 --> 00:24:24.849
decisions and did the same kind of analysis that we would've

00:24:24.849 --> 00:24:28.730
in the private sector, although obviously under a different

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:33.450
spotlight and I think we, as a result, invested the tax

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:36.210
payer dollars wisely.

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:39.549
Thirdly, as I said before, contrary to what some of you may

00:24:39.569 --> 00:24:42.579
have read or think and again, I'm happy to debate it,

00:24:42.599 --> 00:24:46.240
everything we did was not only legal but in accordance with

00:24:46.259 --> 00:24:50.349
precedent and in accordance with, I think, fair treatment of

00:24:50.369 --> 00:24:53.139
all the stakeholders.

00:24:53.159 --> 00:24:58.809
Fourth, as I said, management really matters and I mentioned

00:24:58.829 --> 00:25:04.849
the decision about Rick. We made a similar decision in the

00:25:04.869 --> 00:25:07.379
case of Chrysler that we felt that management was critical

00:25:07.399 --> 00:25:12.000
and a key part of the alliance with Fiat and in deciding to

00:25:12.019 --> 00:25:15.329
partner up with Fiat was the fact that Sergio Marcioni was

00:25:15.349 --> 00:25:19.659
the CEO of Fiat and Sergio, who was not also a lifelong auto

00:25:19.679 --> 00:25:25.379
guy had done a great job of turning Fiat around which had

00:25:25.399 --> 00:25:29.159
been a very troubled auto company. It had actually done very

00:25:29.179 --> 00:25:34.619
poorly in the US in particular which they exited, but Sergio

00:25:34.639 --> 00:25:37.889
had done a great job and we really felt that Sergio was a

00:25:37.889 --> 00:25:41.269
jockey that we could bet on. And so, we now in Detroit have

00:25:41.289 --> 00:25:47.039
this rather interesting situation of the so-called big three

00:25:47.059 --> 00:25:49.449
which they now call the Detroit Three because they are not

00:25:49.449 --> 00:25:53.500
so big anymore are all being run by people who are not from

00:25:53.519 --> 00:25:57.339
Detroit. Two of whom Mulally and Dan Akerson, who is the

00:25:57.359 --> 00:26:00.359
current CEO of GM are not auto guys and Sergio, who is

00:26:00.369 --> 00:26:03.929
certainly not a typical auto guy or a lifelong auto guy. And

00:26:03.949 --> 00:26:09.200
so, even though we certainly got a lot of blowback from the

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:12.139
auto community about who are we a bunch of non-auto guys,

00:26:12.139 --> 00:26:16.690
and who is Akerson and all this kind of stuff, I think that

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:21.319
having fresh blood in this industry has turned out to be the

00:26:21.339 --> 00:26:24.950
right decision. Bill Ford, who I only met after this was all

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:29.210
over, because Ford really was not under our care, gave a

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:32.450
speech in about I think in 2006, '07, right about time we

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:35.559
brought in Mulally which I mentioned in the book in which he

00:26:35.579 --> 00:26:38.839
basically said, "We are an ancilar company and an ancilar

00:26:38.859 --> 00:26:41.859
industry in an ancilar town," and it was something that very

00:26:41.879 --> 00:26:45.960
few people in Detroit would acknowledge or recognize but

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:48.329
Bill Ford did and made that decision to bring in Alan

00:26:48.329 --> 00:26:51.089
Mulally and it was a really smart decision, it was a very

00:26:51.089 --> 00:26:55.269
humble decision and I think it was a very important moment

00:26:55.289 --> 00:27:00.169
of introspection on Bill's part.

00:27:00.189 --> 00:27:03.909
The fifth point I just want to leave you with which is more

00:27:03.929 --> 00:27:06.240
of a Washington point but I assume some of you are

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:08.919
interested in or all of you hopefully are interested in how

00:27:08.919 --> 00:27:13.529
our political process works is that we could not have done

00:27:13.549 --> 00:27:16.909
what we did without the existence of TARP. I'm sure you all

00:27:16.929 --> 00:27:23.899
know what TARP is, the $700 billion financial rescue fund

00:27:23.919 --> 00:27:26.829
that was passed actually President Bush's watch, even though

00:27:26.849 --> 00:27:31.980
President Obama now gets blamed for it but it was probably

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:34.849
the single - I call it the single most important piece of

00:27:34.869 --> 00:27:38.990
economic legislation in certainly my lifetime because it

00:27:39.009 --> 00:27:42.419
gave the Secretary of the Treasury the ability to deploy

00:27:42.439 --> 00:27:45.309
$700 billion. It was actually never all used. I think only

00:27:45.329 --> 00:27:49.159
$450 billion was used to save the financial sector and to

00:27:49.179 --> 00:27:55.839
save the auto sector where we but $82 billion. Without TARP,

00:27:55.859 --> 00:27:59.369
either administration, both administrations would've had to

00:27:59.389 --> 00:28:04.480
have gone to Capitol Hill for every appropriation, for every

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:06.789
dollar that went into the banks or that went into the auto

00:28:06.789 --> 00:28:10.690
companies and having now spent a good bit of time in

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:14.119
Washington, I have to doubt that the Congress would've never

00:28:14.139 --> 00:28:16.629
pulled itself together to appropriate that money in any kind

00:28:16.629 --> 00:28:20.349
of timely way and you would've had a meltdown in the banking

00:28:20.369 --> 00:28:22.659
sector, we would've not had the money to save the auto

00:28:22.659 --> 00:28:27.289
sector and all the consequences that I alluded to before

00:28:27.309 --> 00:28:30.279
about Chrysler would've happened except multiplied.

00:28:30.299 --> 00:28:32.449
I'm not here to tell you that we shouldn't have democracy,

00:28:32.449 --> 00:28:38.179
I'm not here to say that we don't need a Congress but it was

00:28:38.199 --> 00:28:42.919
a real eye opener for me how difficult working with Congress

00:28:42.939 --> 00:28:46.759
can be and how hard it is to get stuff done and how much

00:28:46.779 --> 00:28:49.839
when people blame the President, either President, any

00:28:49.859 --> 00:28:53.279
President for the problems of this country. Frankly, they

00:28:53.299 --> 00:28:55.750
should be turning a lot of their attention to Congress

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:59.159
because the President really has much less power than you

00:28:59.179 --> 00:29:06.509
would imagine. Our founding fathers had a purpose in mind in

00:29:06.529 --> 00:29:10.179
establishing Congress at the Capitol, on the top of the Hill

00:29:10.199 --> 00:29:13.199
and the President in the White House at the bottom of the

00:29:13.199 --> 00:29:15.449
Hill, that wasn't a complete accident. It's just something

00:29:15.449 --> 00:29:18.409
we all need to think about. Congress mostly left us alone,

00:29:18.429 --> 00:29:22.359
happily. When they finally focused on autos, it was only

00:29:22.379 --> 00:29:26.720
because of the dealers. We closed a lot of dealers and

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:31.259
Congress infuriated by that and so in the summer of 2009,

00:29:31.279 --> 00:29:34.259
they spent a lot of time having hearings and calling us up

00:29:34.259 --> 00:29:37.009
to explain why this dealer was being closed or that dealer

00:29:37.009 --> 00:29:39.759
was being closed, stuff that we actually had nothing to do

00:29:39.759 --> 00:29:42.509
with and that was being done thoughtfully, but it was sort

00:29:42.509 --> 00:29:45.009
of an odd juxtaposition that the onetime Congress decided to

00:29:45.009 --> 00:29:49.429
focus on autos it was over dealers, which in our minds were

00:29:49.449 --> 00:29:51.659
not the essential part of it.

00:29:51.679 --> 00:29:57.839
The last point I wanted to leave you with is that this was

00:29:57.859 --> 00:30:01.029
really the first time I had spent a lot of time in the

00:30:01.029 --> 00:30:03.409
manufacturing sector. Clay and I did media and telecom stuff

00:30:03.409 --> 00:30:08.909
at Quad Wrangle's, most of what I did in my career and we

00:30:08.929 --> 00:30:11.179
can have a whole discussion about the manufacturing sector

00:30:11.179 --> 00:30:17.179
but I did come away really very much appreciating and even

00:30:17.199 --> 00:30:21.349
scared about the state of our manufacturing sector; really

00:30:21.369 --> 00:30:25.720
not through our fault but we are competing in a global world

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:28.980
now. We're competing against other countries that are very

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:31.909
good and getting better and better at doing a lot of things

00:30:31.909 --> 00:30:37.409
that we used to be at our own level in doing and it's had

00:30:37.429 --> 00:30:40.000
its impact on our manufacturing sector. In my opinion, it's

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:41.839
going to continue to.

00:30:41.859 --> 00:30:44.899
I'll give you just a couple of quick little anecdotes that

00:30:44.919 --> 00:30:48.059
struck me in the course of this that I talk about in the

00:30:48.059 --> 00:30:51.220
book. One was talking to Fritz Henderson who was the CEO of

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:57.960
GM after Rick for a while and GM had plants in Mexico, has a

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:01.000
very thriving business China, it has operations in India and

00:31:01.019 --> 00:31:04.189
I said, "Fritz, what do you pay outside the US?" So in the

00:31:04.189 --> 00:31:09.019
US, the base wage for UAW worker is $28/hour. In Mexico,

00:31:09.039 --> 00:31:12.799
they pay $7/hour, in China they pay $4/hour and in India

00:31:12.819 --> 00:31:16.059
they pay $1/hour. And I said to Fritz, "So, what's your

00:31:16.059 --> 00:31:18.929
productivity like in Mexico?" and he said, "It's at least as

00:31:18.929 --> 00:31:22.589
good as the US, maybe better." And so, this is what we're up

00:31:22.609 --> 00:31:23.609
against.

00:31:23.609 --> 00:31:27.740
The other vignette that stuck in my mind that I talk about

00:31:27.759 --> 00:31:31.069
in here was one day; I think it was in March sometime,

00:31:31.089 --> 00:31:33.859
Delphi came in to see us, the executives of Delphi. Delphi,

00:31:33.879 --> 00:31:37.599
as you all probably know, was GM's parts operation. It was

00:31:37.619 --> 00:31:41.279
spun off by GM in 1999. It went bankrupt about three or four

00:31:41.299 --> 00:31:44.200
years ago. It was sort of sitting in bankruptcy for several

00:31:44.220 --> 00:31:47.179
years and they came in as one of the many suppliers who came

00:31:47.179 --> 00:31:50.119
in to see us to tell us they were being crushed by the

00:31:50.119 --> 00:31:53.159
problems at GM and Chrysler and could we help them? And so,

00:31:53.179 --> 00:31:56.319
they were kind of making their case for help and Delphi is

00:31:56.339 --> 00:31:59.639
based in Troy, Michigan. They were very earnest, solid Mid-

00:31:59.659 --> 00:32:03.609
Western kind of folks and I'm listening and I don't know why

00:32:03.629 --> 00:32:06.899
it popped into my head but at one moment I said, "How many

00:32:06.919 --> 00:32:10.119
people do you guys employ?" The numbers are here but the

00:32:10.139 --> 00:32:13.339
answer was something like 135000 and I said, "How many of

00:32:13.359 --> 00:32:17.269
them are in the US?" and they said, "15000", so Delphi, this

00:32:17.289 --> 00:32:20.980
quintessential American company had quietly, carefully,

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:25.369
deliberately moved 90% of its workers offshore.

00:32:25.389 --> 00:32:29.470
This is the challenge we face in manufacturing and we can,

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:32.689
again, have a lively debate about whether we should do more

00:32:32.709 --> 00:32:36.309
or less to help manufacturing, but I just think we should be

00:32:36.329 --> 00:32:41.869
mindful of the fact that it is a challenge.

00:32:41.889 --> 00:32:44.750
Let me close, because probably some of you are wondering

00:32:44.769 --> 00:32:48.889
what the score card was on our adventures in autoland. I

00:32:48.909 --> 00:32:53.269
mentioned that we invested $82 billion in these companies.

00:32:53.289 --> 00:32:56.829
If you look at where GM stock is trading now, if you look at

00:32:56.849 --> 00:33:01.389
how GM values at stake and

00:33:01.409 --> 00:33:03.209
[inaudible] which actually was our second biggest investment

00:33:03.209 --> 00:33:06.619
after GM, a bit larger than Chrysler, if you look at the

00:33:06.639 --> 00:33:11.359
valuations that are out there on Chrysler equity, US tax

00:33:11.379 --> 00:33:15.220
payer, as we sit here today would recover about 85% of the

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:18.619
money that was put up. In other words, a loss of potentially

00:33:18.639 --> 00:33:20.699
around $10 billion.

00:33:20.719 --> 00:33:24.079
I continue to believe that there is a lot of upside in the

00:33:24.099 --> 00:33:27.849
sector. It's had a bump in the road, no pun intended; in the

00:33:27.869 --> 00:33:30.529
last few weeks because of the oil price stuff, shares are

00:33:30.529 --> 00:33:34.779
down but I think the general trajectory will continue to be

00:33:34.799 --> 00:33:37.949
up and so I think we can actually do better than that, but

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:42.960
even if we end up losing $10 billion, I would argue that $10

00:33:42.980 --> 00:33:47.349
billion was money well spent to save the sector because no

00:33:47.369 --> 00:33:50.359
only would we have lost Chrysler, we would have lost GM, we

00:33:50.359 --> 00:33:52.809
would've lost the suppliers, we would've lost actually Ford

00:33:52.809 --> 00:33:55.659
because they wouldn't be able to get parts at least for a

00:33:55.659 --> 00:33:59.769
while and it would've been an economic calamity akin to what

00:33:59.789 --> 00:34:02.289
would've happened if we hadn't saved the banking sector and

00:34:02.289 --> 00:34:06.269
I think devastation across much of the Mid-West.

00:34:06.289 --> 00:34:09.739
I'm proud of what we did, I'm proud of the President's

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:12.989
decision to do what he did and glad to be here to tell the

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:16.099
story and happy to spend what time we have left on questions

00:34:16.119 --> 00:34:19.049
or comments. Sir -

00:34:19.069 --> 00:34:28.090
[Inaudible] to what extent you played out and what would

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:30.360
happen under new scenario, because my intuition would say

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:33.360
that we wouldn't stand to lose all that; like the assets are

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:36.360
not going to sit there. They are going to be bought up

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:39.579
either by foreign auto companies or Ford, part suppliers

00:34:39.599 --> 00:34:46.019
still need to supply and there is a certain intrinsic demand

00:34:46.039 --> 00:34:48.539
for autos, so to what extent do you have this

00:34:48.539 --> 00:34:50.539
[inaudible] different ownership and how does that change the

00:34:50.539 --> 00:34:51.789
decisions that you guys made?

00:34:51.789 --> 00:34:57.039
That's a good question. With respect to the overall decision

00:34:57.059 --> 00:35:02.670
to intervene, we honestly didn't have the time and we were

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:05.980
too terrified of what the result would be to sort of say

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:09.150
what would happen if we did nothing. I just gave you a real

00:35:09.150 --> 00:35:12.150
capsule at the end of what I think would've happened if we

00:35:12.150 --> 00:35:16.659
had done nothing which would've been GM and Chrysler would'

00:35:16.679 --> 00:35:20.179
ve run out of money at the end of March of 2009. They would'

00:35:20.179 --> 00:35:22.429
ve closed their doors, they would've stopped paying people.

00:35:22.429 --> 00:35:37.340
You've got all these grants. They told you that they would

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:40.110
close their doors, just like them without a plan. Do you

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:43.840
think that the CEO

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:44.860
[inaudible].

00:35:44.860 --> 00:35:58.980
Can I respond to that? So you knew exactly when they were

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:00.500
going to run out of money?

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:01.500
No, we didn't actually.

00:36:01.500 --> 00:36:02.750
You had no idea when?

00:36:02.750 --> 00:36:05.250
From having seen the very knitty gritty balance sheets of

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:07.250
these businesses, they had absolutely no idea how much cash

00:36:07.250 --> 00:36:08.250
they had.

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:11.000
The alternative is, he knew the government was going to bail

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:13.500
him out, so he needed to spend all that money...

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:26.179
The CFO of GM could not any given day tell us within $500

00:36:26.199 --> 00:36:29.139
million how much cash they had. They did not have the

00:36:29.159 --> 00:36:32.420
systems to know. As a result, they said they needed $11

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:36.509
billion of cash to operate and they should've been able to

00:36:36.529 --> 00:36:39.460
operate $6 billion of cash or some number like that. They

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:44.610
didn't know. They really didn't know.

00:36:44.630 --> 00:36:55.900
Do you know? I mean you just told us you are not an auto

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:59.170
guy. Do you know more than the guys who were running it all

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:01.420
their life. It just doesn't seem to be very -

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:04.920
I don't know how many times or how many days but I can tell

00:37:04.920 --> 00:37:07.170
you, they didn't have any money. They could not have paid

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:08.170
their people.

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:09.920
I'm sure they didn't have money, but they did that

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:10.920
intentionally.

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:12.670
You think that they did that intentionally?

00:37:12.670 --> 00:37:14.670
You were willing to hand them extra money.

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:17.420
You think that GM basically ended 2007 with $30 billion of

00:37:17.420 --> 00:37:20.170
cash and spent all that money on purpose so that the

00:37:20.170 --> 00:37:21.420
government would bail them out.

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:23.420
You've got $30 billion. Think about it, you've got $30

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:24.420
billion, pay

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:25.420
[inaudible]

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:26.670
Sir, with all due respect, you hijacked my question.

00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:27.670
[Laughter]

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:29.670
In fairness to everybody else who paid the $12 to come to

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:39.159
this meeting, why don't I answer this gentleman's question?

00:37:39.179 --> 00:37:41.699
The answer is they would've closed their doors, they would

00:37:41.699 --> 00:37:44.369
not have been able to pay their people, they would've all

00:37:44.369 --> 00:37:49.319
lost their jobs that day. The suppliers were actually in

00:37:49.339 --> 00:37:51.839
worse shape than GM and Chrysler because their business had

00:37:51.839 --> 00:37:54.949
fallen off just as much but they hadn't got the $17 billion

00:37:54.949 --> 00:37:58.599
at the end of 2008, so that's why we heard from the

00:37:58.619 --> 00:38:01.539
suppliers. Actually, first thing we in fact did was the

00:38:01.559 --> 00:38:03.809
supplier kind of guarantee program which was frankly more

00:38:03.809 --> 00:38:06.589
optics than substance because we didn't feel we could help

00:38:06.609 --> 00:38:09.109
every company in this industry, but the suppliers were in

00:38:09.109 --> 00:38:11.889
tough shape and most of them actually did end up going

00:38:11.909 --> 00:38:13.679
bankrupt but restructuring in bankruptcy, but they would've

00:38:13.679 --> 00:38:16.699
closed their doors. The dealers would've temporarily closed

00:38:16.719 --> 00:38:21.769
their doors and we can debate the number, but I think we

00:38:21.789 --> 00:38:24.039
would've had a couple of million people out of work very,

00:38:24.039 --> 00:38:25.039
very quickly.

00:38:25.039 --> 00:38:28.289
Now, your point is completely right and we did do a lot of

00:38:28.289 --> 00:38:30.539
analysis around Chrysler on this point that obviously people

00:38:30.539 --> 00:38:32.969
would've picked up some of that business. For example, and

00:38:32.969 --> 00:38:35.969
Clay will help me, I think roughly half of the business of a

00:38:35.969 --> 00:38:38.719
typical car dealership is actually repairs, it's not selling

00:38:38.739 --> 00:38:41.509
new cars. So obviously, people were going to still have

00:38:41.529 --> 00:38:44.029
their cars repaired so some of these people would've kept

00:38:44.029 --> 00:38:47.029
their jobs and would've been hired back. Some part of a car

00:38:47.029 --> 00:38:49.029
dealership is selling used cars, that would've continued.

00:38:49.029 --> 00:38:51.529
You are correct that for example, the Jeep line that

00:38:51.529 --> 00:38:55.139
Chrysler had we thought was worth a fair amount of money and

00:38:55.159 --> 00:38:57.980
would be sold to some European but would they keep making

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:01.150
Jeep in the US or would they simply take the name plate and

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:03.900
make the cars wherever they happened to be based and send

00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:06.900
the cars to the US, who knew? So, you're right that there

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:11.369
would've been a gradual process. You would've had a onetime

00:39:11.389 --> 00:39:15.259
loss of this huge number of jobs and then a gradual process

00:39:15.279 --> 00:39:19.139
of the jobs coming back in one form or another, because as

00:39:19.159 --> 00:39:22.079
you implied in your question, the number of cars that would

00:39:22.099 --> 00:39:25.099
be sold in US would probably not change very much, it was

00:39:25.099 --> 00:39:28.349
just a question of who was going to sell them, but in our

00:39:28.349 --> 00:39:30.849
view it would've been a long painful process. There would've

00:39:30.849 --> 00:39:33.190
been certainly a permanent loss of American jobs because

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:35.960
Ford was in no position. Ford was worried they themselves

00:39:35.980 --> 00:39:38.449
were going to go out of business. They were in no position

00:39:38.449 --> 00:39:41.929
to buy anything. So it probably would've been some non-US

00:39:41.949 --> 00:39:45.339
entity and we don't know at the end of it how many jobs

00:39:45.339 --> 00:39:48.269
would've remained, just like we don't know if we had not

00:39:48.269 --> 00:39:50.969
bailed out AIG we don't know what would happen. If we had

00:39:50.969 --> 00:39:53.969
not put in the TARP money into those banks on Columbus Day,

00:39:53.969 --> 00:39:56.719
that Hank Paulson put the money into, we don't know what

00:39:56.719 --> 00:39:59.219
would've happened. We just felt it was an unacceptable risk

00:39:59.219 --> 00:40:02.219
to take, not some kind of social experiment to say let's let

00:40:02.219 --> 00:40:05.469
Chrysler and GM go out of business or run out of money and

00:40:05.469 --> 00:40:08.219
then we'll see what happens. We just didn't feel that was

00:40:08.219 --> 00:40:10.719
the responsible outcome and we had an alternative that we

00:40:10.719 --> 00:40:15.389
felt was financially viable and relatively small financial

00:40:15.409 --> 00:40:16.900
exposure for the American tax payers.

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:25.559
Thanks for coming. You alluded a little bit to the potential

00:40:25.579 --> 00:40:27.579
fire struck that the administration had over this

00:40:27.579 --> 00:40:33.179
[inaudible] that Unions got over the bond holders. Can you

00:40:33.199 --> 00:40:42.880
talk a little bit more about the politics and giving up the

00:40:42.900 --> 00:40:44.900
restructuring and dealing with bond holders and how that all

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:45.900
came out?

00:40:45.900 --> 00:40:48.400
Can you explain how it's consistent with the bond investment

00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:49.400
while you're at it?

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:51.650
Okay. When people raise that issue, they are generally

00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:53.400
talking about Chrysler and Chrysler's capital structure

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:59.000
essentially consisted of $6.8 billion of so called secured

00:40:59.019 --> 00:41:02.019
debt that was actually a bunch of banks. It was six or seven

00:41:02.019 --> 00:41:04.269
banks. There were no bond holders actually technically in

00:41:04.269 --> 00:41:06.960
Chrysler and then there was some second and third lien debt

00:41:06.960 --> 00:41:09.909
that was held by little bit by Daimler, little bit by

00:41:09.929 --> 00:41:12.849
Cerberus, a bunch by the US government and then obviously

00:41:12.869 --> 00:41:17.949
there was equity. So, the challenge with Chrysler was that

00:41:17.969 --> 00:41:21.719
the $6.8 billion of senior secured debt was not really

00:41:21.739 --> 00:41:25.139
secured by anything. It was sort of notionally secured but

00:41:25.159 --> 00:41:27.659
Chrysler didn't have $6.8 billion worth of assets. Chrysler'

00:41:27.659 --> 00:41:32.880
s value in liquidation was $1 billion or less in our opinion

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:38.429
and JP Morgan would agree with this.

00:41:38.449 --> 00:41:42.509
Essentially, the first question was what was the fair

00:41:42.529 --> 00:41:46.659
recovery for those banks? The debt was trading at 15 cents

00:41:46.679 --> 00:41:49.349
on the dollar, that's roughly a billion dollar valuation. As

00:41:49.349 --> 00:41:52.539
I said, our view was that if we simply handed the keys to

00:41:52.539 --> 00:41:56.329
those banks which is one way to handle a bankrupt situation,

00:41:56.349 --> 00:41:58.329
they would've knocked out more than a billion dollars out

00:41:58.329 --> 00:42:02.000
and probably they've acknowledged and when I talked to them

00:42:02.019 --> 00:42:04.199
[inaudible], they acknowledged it was actually probably less

00:42:04.219 --> 00:42:07.759
they would've gotten out. So we paid them in the end $2

00:42:07.779 --> 00:42:12.650
billion of cash basically to go home and it's also normal in

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:16.339
a bankruptcy as you all probably know for the banks not to

00:42:16.359 --> 00:42:19.170
get cash but to get more paper, so we actually were

00:42:19.190 --> 00:42:24.019
incredibly generous with those banks relative to what they

00:42:24.039 --> 00:42:27.710
were entitled to. And in fact, Jamie Diamond at JP Morgan

00:42:27.730 --> 00:42:30.799
when I talked him about this months after he said to me, "My

00:42:30.799 --> 00:42:33.109
problem is not what we've got. I think we were treated

00:42:33.109 --> 00:42:35.589
fairly. My problem is with what the UAW got."

00:42:35.609 --> 00:42:39.659
So, let's turn to the UAW and I would start by arguing that

00:42:39.679 --> 00:42:42.409
frankly, what UAW got is none of his business. In other

00:42:42.409 --> 00:42:46.269
words, he got at least as much, I would argue, substantially

00:42:46.289 --> 00:42:49.079
more than he was entitled to get or would've gotten under

00:42:49.079 --> 00:42:53.319
any other circumstances. We made a decision; the UAW had a

00:42:53.339 --> 00:42:57.860
$12 billion claim against Chrysler for what was called a

00:42:57.880 --> 00:43:00.779
VEBA, it was for the famous retiree health care benefits

00:43:00.799 --> 00:43:03.159
that you've all read a lot about. The company has actually

00:43:03.159 --> 00:43:06.889
to their credit had done a good job before we showed up of

00:43:06.909 --> 00:43:08.969
putting a box around these open-ended health care

00:43:08.969 --> 00:43:12.509
liabilities and by doing a deal with the UAW in which they

00:43:12.529 --> 00:43:15.940
both agreed to put a fixed amount of money into a trust that

00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:18.679
would pay these health care costs and in return for that,

00:43:18.679 --> 00:43:21.489
that would be all the companies had to pay. There could be

00:43:21.489 --> 00:43:24.190
no further retiree health care liability to companies.

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:27.489
In Chrysler's case, it was a $12 billion obligation. They

00:43:27.509 --> 00:43:34.529
had funded about $1 billion I think and I think there was

00:43:34.549 --> 00:43:36.799
$12 billion left. It's an unsecured claim against the

00:43:36.799 --> 00:43:39.049
company. We could've wiped it out. We could've basically

00:43:39.049 --> 00:43:41.549
said, "You're down in the capital structure," I hope this

00:43:41.549 --> 00:43:44.119
all makes sense to you guys. I assume you're in business

00:43:44.119 --> 00:43:50.309
school, hopefully it does. So we said we could've wiped that

00:43:50.329 --> 00:43:57.769
out but we felt that if we did that we would simply not have

00:43:57.789 --> 00:44:01.829
workers to do this job, that essentially this was all part

00:44:01.849 --> 00:44:06.170
of a negotiation and in order to reach some we were the UAW

00:44:06.190 --> 00:44:09.659
to make a lot of sacrifices but asking them to wipe out all

00:44:09.679 --> 00:44:13.380
the retiree health care funds which would affect obviously

00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:15.599
the retirees but also the people who were still working

00:44:15.599 --> 00:44:19.190
there was simply a bridge too far and so we converted half

00:44:19.210 --> 00:44:23.480
of that into a note and gave them equity for the other half.

00:44:23.500 --> 00:44:26.400
And that happened to be 55% of the equity of the company

00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:29.179
underneath a huge mountain of debt that was still sitting on

00:44:29.199 --> 00:44:33.339
top of it, of US government debt and other debt and our view

00:44:33.359 --> 00:44:36.900
was that that equity was not worth a huge amount at that

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:40.170
point in time. We hoped it would be worth more later but we

00:44:40.170 --> 00:44:43.670
didn't put a lot of value on it at that moment but we felt

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:46.779
it was something that was important to do to have an

00:44:46.799 --> 00:44:49.799
acceptable range with the UAW. And there is precedent for

00:44:49.819 --> 00:44:55.239
this and my colleague Ron Bloom, my deputy had done a whole

00:44:55.259 --> 00:44:58.500
bunch of steel industry bankruptcies and many of those

00:44:58.519 --> 00:45:03.519
actually had VEBAs like Chrysler and in some of those cases

00:45:03.539 --> 00:45:06.329
those VEBAs also got equity in the company as part of their

00:45:06.329 --> 00:45:10.199
compensation and the restructuring. So, it was litigated all

00:45:10.219 --> 00:45:13.319
the way through bankruptcy court, all through the court of

00:45:13.339 --> 00:45:16.089
appeals, all the way to the United States Supreme Court and

00:45:16.089 --> 00:45:20.549
essentially every judge said what we said, which was that

00:45:20.569 --> 00:45:24.789
the key element is that every creditor got at least as much

00:45:24.809 --> 00:45:27.809
or more than they were entitled to get or would've gotten in

00:45:27.809 --> 00:45:31.139
a normal bankruptcy and the fact that some got this much

00:45:31.159 --> 00:45:32.949
more than they would've gotten and some only got this much

00:45:32.949 --> 00:45:36.809
more than they would've gotten isn't really a litigatable

00:45:36.829 --> 00:45:40.380
issue. Everybody got at least as much or more than they

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:43.150
would've gotten and that's why we thought it was fair, and

00:45:43.150 --> 00:45:46.150
there were no politics around it. Honest to God, I was never

00:45:46.170 --> 00:45:50.869
told by the White House to do anything about the UAW other

00:45:50.889 --> 00:45:53.989
than what we did. In fact, at one point Ron Gettelfinger

00:45:54.009 --> 00:45:58.109
wanted to go in and see the President Rahm Emanuel and

00:45:58.129 --> 00:46:01.599
basically they said, "Look, the auto team is doing this and

00:46:01.619 --> 00:46:02.619
deal with them." Yes, Ma'am -

00:46:02.619 --> 00:46:05.099
Thank you. I'm curious as to how your team approached

00:46:05.099 --> 00:46:07.849
setting a new precedent as you went through this. You talked

00:46:07.849 --> 00:46:18.130
a little bit about looking backwards at this had a precedent

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:21.400
but how did you think about what you were doing at the time

00:46:21.400 --> 00:46:23.400
being a potential future model for similar cases?

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:27.109
Actually, we thought about it a lot and Larry Summers

00:46:27.129 --> 00:46:30.000
particularly thought about in our behalf; maybe in the

00:46:30.019 --> 00:46:33.829
opposite of the way you mean your question which was, we

00:46:33.849 --> 00:46:36.309
really did not want to set a precedent for the government

00:46:36.309 --> 00:46:40.569
intervening in the private sector. We all thought we were

00:46:40.589 --> 00:46:45.949
pretty smart, we all thought we knew what we were doing but

00:46:45.969 --> 00:46:50.000
none of us, I'm sure we didn't know as much as we thought we

00:46:50.019 --> 00:46:53.219
did but in any event, none of us liked the idea that the

00:46:53.219 --> 00:46:55.079
next group and the next group and the next group and any

00:46:55.079 --> 00:46:58.980
industry could come in and say, "Do for us what you did for

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:01.500
the car companies," and get government money, we hated that

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:04.529
idea just the way we hated what my Vietnam analogy, just the

00:47:04.549 --> 00:47:07.299
way we hated the idea of having a long term ownership

00:47:07.299 --> 00:47:09.690
position in these companies, which is part of why GM went

00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:12.549
public so quickly was for the government to be able to sell.

00:47:12.549 --> 00:47:16.309
We viewed ourselves as investors of last resort. If there

00:47:16.329 --> 00:47:19.039
had been any private capital that wanted to come in instead

00:47:19.039 --> 00:47:21.809
of us, we would've said it's all yours. We didn't want to be

00:47:21.809 --> 00:47:26.529
here or to do this. And so, our goal was to leave as light a

00:47:26.549 --> 00:47:30.429
footprint as we could to be in and out as quickly as we

00:47:30.449 --> 00:47:33.279
could, to have as little to do with running these companies

00:47:33.299 --> 00:47:36.049
as we could, to have these private sector boards and frankly

00:47:36.049 --> 00:47:39.190
to set as little a precedent as we could for future

00:47:39.210 --> 00:47:42.750
involvement, because we believe rightly or wrongly, even as

00:47:42.769 --> 00:47:45.549
Democrats, that the government to the maximum extent

00:47:45.569 --> 00:47:48.980
possible should stay out of the private sector, especially

00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:52.920
the industrial sector. We didn't believe that any of us had

00:47:52.940 --> 00:47:56.039
the wisdom to override the kind of decisions of investors

00:47:56.059 --> 00:47:59.460
and the market place and capitalists who were allocating

00:47:59.480 --> 00:48:01.009
capital. We didn't thing the government should be doing

00:48:01.009 --> 00:48:02.009
that. Yes, sir.

00:48:02.009 --> 00:48:05.009
There was a case in 1980 that also applied for a government

00:48:05.009 --> 00:48:07.069
bailout and received it. To what extent were you influenced

00:48:07.069 --> 00:48:08.069
by that?

00:48:08.069 --> 00:48:16.789
There are few cases. Chrysler, I think it was actually '79

00:48:16.809 --> 00:48:20.730
or maybe it was '80 and Lockheed is one, Con Rail. There are

00:48:20.750 --> 00:48:23.569
certainly a few precedents for the government stepping in

00:48:23.589 --> 00:48:26.259
under extreme circumstances. We were not particularly

00:48:26.279 --> 00:48:30.079
influenced by that early Chrysler precedent and I would

00:48:30.099 --> 00:48:33.199
argue that each of those; I don't know enough about them to

00:48:33.199 --> 00:48:35.559
evaluate whether they were the right or the wrong thing, I

00:48:35.559 --> 00:48:39.159
would argue that I hope the people who made those decisions

00:48:39.179 --> 00:48:42.319
in those cases basically made the same decision we made

00:48:42.339 --> 00:48:46.000
which was systemic risk call it too big to fail, call it

00:48:46.019 --> 00:48:50.880
what you want, that it was just too much of a risk to put on

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:55.539
the economy at that particular moment in time but again, I

00:48:55.559 --> 00:48:59.250
think the number of cases like that is a small handful and I

00:48:59.269 --> 00:49:01.769
think it should stay a small handful and I think it should

00:49:01.769 --> 00:49:04.589
only happen under the most extreme circumstances and

00:49:04.609 --> 00:49:07.000
hopefully, part of what I've tried to say in the book, what

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:10.250
I try to say in these talks and what we've said publicly and

00:49:10.250 --> 00:49:13.250
the President said publicly is we just want this to be the

00:49:13.250 --> 00:49:18.139
exception and not to have people feel this is not the long

00:49:18.159 --> 00:49:20.969
arm of government, this is not creeping socialism, this is

00:49:20.989 --> 00:49:24.670
not Obama Motors, this is sort of a decision that we had to

00:49:24.690 --> 00:49:26.920
make but Bush, we hadn't have to make.

00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:27.920
[Informal Talk]

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:30.289
Thank you for coming. Looking back on some of the past

00:49:30.309 --> 00:49:32.059
government policies and protection policies; from an

00:49:32.059 --> 00:49:34.809
outsider view do you think that some of these policies are

00:49:34.809 --> 00:49:44.119
put in Chrysler and GM situation

00:49:44.139 --> 00:49:45.139
[inaudible]?

00:49:44.139 --> 00:49:47.210
Like what kind of policies?

00:49:47.230 --> 00:49:52.670
Some of the trading agreements, protecting agreements they

00:49:52.690 --> 00:49:53.690
had specifically in

00:49:53.690 --> 00:50:03.569
[inaudible] went against Bush's free trade agreement and

00:50:03.589 --> 00:50:05.839
business protection there, do you think that they are as

00:50:05.839 --> 00:50:06.839
managed as those?

00:50:06.839 --> 00:50:10.089
The whole thing; when I was a report at the New York times

00:50:10.089 --> 00:50:21.199
actually I covered a little bit the crisis or bailout in '79

00:50:21.219 --> 00:50:26.319
or '80 and during that period there was a lot of debate

00:50:26.339 --> 00:50:29.440
about the Japanese and whether it was fair trade or free

00:50:29.460 --> 00:50:32.460
trade and whether we would have kind of been taken for kind

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:36.129
of suckers or not and in fact, part of the reason that

00:50:36.149 --> 00:50:38.659
Japanese started assembling cars here was that they were

00:50:38.679 --> 00:50:41.679
afraid that we were going to put in import quotas on them

00:50:41.679 --> 00:50:46.029
and therefore they'd be shut out of the market and so, one

00:50:46.049 --> 00:50:48.920
of the reasons why they started assembling here was to avoid

00:50:48.940 --> 00:50:51.449
that possibility.

00:50:51.469 --> 00:50:54.449
Look, one of the things and this gets back to my management

00:50:54.449 --> 00:50:58.719
matters theme of that article that you all have, one of the

00:50:58.739 --> 00:51:02.679
things that struck me in the course of this is the fact that

00:51:02.699 --> 00:51:08.069
GM had this huge problem. We put $50 billion into GM. GM

00:51:08.089 --> 00:51:10.929
actually turned out in some ways worse shape than Chrysler

00:51:10.949 --> 00:51:15.329
even though it's much bigger kind of company proportionate

00:51:15.349 --> 00:51:18.539
to its revenues it got more capital than Chrysler and so,

00:51:18.559 --> 00:51:21.960
here you have GM that runs for $30 billion in 15 months and

00:51:21.980 --> 00:51:25.259
literally did show up having run out of money. Ford did get

00:51:25.279 --> 00:51:30.609
into the trouble as I said; Ford had a tough brush, but Ford

00:51:30.629 --> 00:51:34.909
got through it and Ford had huge profits last year, near

00:51:34.929 --> 00:51:38.559
records profits last year and never needed a government

00:51:38.579 --> 00:51:41.750
handout. And so, the question is why?

00:51:41.769 --> 00:51:44.960
They are playing exactly the same set of cards, right? Same

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:47.579
UAW contacts, same Japanese competition or South Korean

00:51:47.599 --> 00:51:51.929
competition, same oil prices, same credit crisis; so why is

00:51:51.949 --> 00:51:55.509
Ford doing so well and Jim isn't. The only conclusion I can

00:51:55.529 --> 00:51:59.079
come to is management. So, I can't tell you that every

00:51:59.099 --> 00:52:01.679
policy decision the government made over the last 40 years

00:52:01.699 --> 00:52:04.690
about autos helped or hurt whether it was right or wrong,

00:52:04.710 --> 00:52:07.710
but I do think that a fair amount of the trouble that

00:52:07.710 --> 00:52:10.710
Detroit got into was of its own making and the difference in

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:13.460
how the three companies went through it does reflect the

00:52:13.480 --> 00:52:16.139
quality of management and the strategic decisions made the

00:52:16.159 --> 00:52:17.159
long way. Yes, sir.

00:52:17.159 --> 00:52:19.909
If it's true that management that you say that sort of

00:52:19.909 --> 00:52:22.159
distinguishes whether an auto company is going to succeed,

00:52:22.159 --> 00:52:24.659
why didn't the private sector step in earlier? I could

00:52:24.659 --> 00:52:32.349
imagine like a private investor recognizing that there is a

00:52:32.369 --> 00:52:34.369
depressing value in this particular management stepping in.

00:52:34.369 --> 00:52:37.119
Is there any inefficiency in our take over market here that

00:52:37.119 --> 00:52:40.069
sort of could have prevented this problem at an earlier

00:52:40.089 --> 00:52:41.089
stage?

00:52:41.089 --> 00:52:44.629
I don't know if it would've prevented the problem. I was

00:52:44.649 --> 00:52:47.899
with you until that last part but we'll come back to it but

00:52:47.899 --> 00:52:50.899
one of the things that Larry Summers and I used to debate

00:52:50.899 --> 00:52:53.679
and I shouldn't be debating about anything but at least in

00:52:53.679 --> 00:52:55.759
this one area I felt like we were kind of on neutral

00:52:55.759 --> 00:52:59.019
territory which was the sort of efficient markets hypothesis

00:52:59.039 --> 00:53:01.710
which actually Larry has argued both sides of it different

00:53:01.730 --> 00:53:07.109
times in his career and I respect markets. I'm a private

00:53:07.129 --> 00:53:11.809
sector guy but I also think markets don't always work and in

00:53:11.829 --> 00:53:14.940
March of 2009, you could've bought Ford stock for a dollar

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:20.750
or something. It's now at $14 something. So there was a case

00:53:20.769 --> 00:53:23.269
where private capital could've said, "This is a great

00:53:23.279 --> 00:53:27.199
opportunity, let's come in," and they would've made a lot of

00:53:27.219 --> 00:53:31.500
money. Even when we put $30 billion into GM; of the $50

00:53:31.519 --> 00:53:33.839
billion $30 billion went in on June 1st when GM went into

00:53:33.839 --> 00:53:37.730
bankruptcy and the first $20 was kind of lost, it has sort

00:53:37.750 --> 00:53:40.250
of fallen over bankruptcy process. That $30 billion was what

00:53:40.250 --> 00:53:43.250
bought us our 60% of the equity. If anybody had showed up

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:46.250
and said, "I want a piece of that $30 billion," we would've

00:53:46.250 --> 00:53:48.750
said, "Great. Here, take it." That $30 billion today is

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:51.879
worth $40 - $45 billion. It's not ten times but it was a

00:53:51.879 --> 00:53:55.449
very good investment that we made on June 1st. So, I think

00:53:55.469 --> 00:53:58.940
markets can be inefficient and this I guess is good

00:53:58.960 --> 00:54:01.699
questions that you all have asked about precedent and about

00:54:01.719 --> 00:54:05.599
when the government should intervene; markets do fail and I

00:54:05.619 --> 00:54:09.420
think we can debate the financial rescue, the bank stuff and

00:54:09.440 --> 00:54:12.199
I have views about all that, but I think broadly speaking,

00:54:12.219 --> 00:54:15.969
there are moments when government does have a role to play

00:54:15.989 --> 00:54:18.650
when markets do fail, when private capital isn't being

00:54:18.670 --> 00:54:22.670
allocated efficiently or intelligently and you see some of

00:54:22.690 --> 00:54:25.440
that going on, Europe is going to face those decisions over

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:28.190
the next few weeks about what they do about Greece and

00:54:28.190 --> 00:54:30.690
Portugal and Ireland, same kinds of decisions. So, they are

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:33.029
tough decisions, they are really tough decisions. I do think

00:54:33.029 --> 00:54:35.500
that both the Bush administration and the Obama

00:54:35.519 --> 00:54:38.230
administration really got most of them right in this case

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:40.440
but I think these kinds of interventions should be the

00:54:40.440 --> 00:54:57.429
exception, not the rule. Yes?

00:54:57.449 --> 00:54:58.449
[Inaudible]

00:54:58.449 --> 00:55:09.449
I do think that the notion of trying to avoid the necessity

00:55:09.469 --> 00:55:12.609
of government ever intervening by reducing every institution

00:55:12.629 --> 00:55:15.440
to whatever it's considered to be the maximum size that

00:55:15.460 --> 00:55:20.699
makes them not systemic is not practical. The example I like

00:55:20.719 --> 00:55:26.509
to use is LTCM in 1998 was not on anybody's list of the 25

00:55:26.529 --> 00:55:30.089
biggest institutions or most important institutions and it

00:55:30.109 --> 00:55:32.549
almost brought down the whole financial system and required

00:55:32.569 --> 00:55:37.219
a fairly dramatic intervention. The Canadian banking sector

00:55:37.239 --> 00:55:41.369
is far more concentrated than our banking sector is and yet

00:55:41.389 --> 00:55:43.710
they got through all this without needing the government

00:55:43.730 --> 00:55:46.109
intervening. So those are two examples on either side of the

00:55:46.109 --> 00:55:47.949
question.

00:55:47.969 --> 00:55:50.839
Look, I think obviously a lot of mistakes were made in

00:55:50.859 --> 00:55:55.650
deregulating the financial sector. It is too integral to be

00:55:55.670 --> 00:55:58.609
allowed to just sort of run a mock and whether the Dodd

00:55:58.609 --> 00:56:01.489
Frank stuff and all the pieces of it, the Velcro and so

00:56:01.489 --> 00:56:04.239
forth are the exactly the right things or not, time will

00:56:04.239 --> 00:56:07.489
tell but I think some of what we did was right but nobody

00:56:07.489 --> 00:56:10.190
should kid themselves that either Dodd Frank or what we did

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:12.980
in autos or breaking these companies down would avoid the

00:56:13.000 --> 00:56:15.629
need for government ever intervene. That's part of why you

00:56:15.629 --> 00:56:18.779
have government, is to deal with extraordinary circumstances

00:56:18.799 --> 00:56:22.009
and I've said a couple of times and I'll say it one last

00:56:22.009 --> 00:56:25.009
time, what we should all hope and pray for is that our

00:56:25.009 --> 00:56:27.569
leaders in the future are thoughtful about when they do

00:56:27.589 --> 00:56:31.529
choose to intervene because I do think it should be by far

00:56:31.549 --> 00:56:34.639
the exception and obviously when you do it, you want to do

00:56:34.659 --> 00:56:37.409
it right as I said, I think both administrations have done

00:56:37.409 --> 00:56:38.909
over the past couple of years.

